    <plot x="710" autoPlotX="true" yMax="100.0" autoPlotY="true" y="221" xMin="0.0" height="205" legend="false" xMax="10.0" yMin="0.0" width="250" display="Percent-required-to-relocate">
      <setup></setup>
      <update></update>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="default" color="-16777216" legend="true">
        <setup></setup>
        <update>plot calculate-percent-required-to-relocate</update>
      </pen>
    </plot>
    <plot x="710" autoPlotX="true" yMax="100.0" autoPlotY="true" yAxis="%" y="11" xMin="0.0" height="205" legend="false" xMax="5.0" yMin="0.0" width="250" xAxis="time" display="Percent Similar">
      <setup></setup>
      <update></update>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="percent" color="-16777216" legend="true">
        <setup></setup>
        <update>plot calculate-percent-similar</update>
      </pen>
    </plot>
    <monitor x="965" precision="1" y="225" height="60" fontSize="11" width="150" display="% required-to-relocate">calculate-percent-required-to-relocate</monitor>
    <monitor x="965" precision="1" y="155" height="60" fontSize="11" width="150" display="% similar">calculate-percent-similar</monitor>
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[# Schelling Segregation Model: Policy Version

## WHAT IS IT?

This course model was adapted by Prof. Jose Magallanes from Uri Wilensky’s NetLogo Segregation model (1997). It examines whether an imposed neighbourhood rule can produce residential mixing and allow relocation to stop.

Citizens belong to one of two groups and occupy residential locations. The policy sets a minimum percentage of occupied neighbours who must belong to the citizen’s own group. Citizens below that minimum must attempt relocation to a randomly selected vacant location. Citizens meeting the requirement remain in place.

The rule represents administrative compliance, not personal happiness or approval. A minimum of 30% allows citizens to remain with 30%, 60%, or even 100% same-group neighbours. It does not require balanced neighbourhoods.

The model represents a residential mechanism. It does not represent housing prices, enforcement institutions, moving costs, revolt, or political support.

## HOW TO USE IT

Use a **51 × 51 world**, with coordinates −25 through 25 on both axes, and turn off horizontal and vertical wrapping. These are world settings; `setup` does not set the world size or topology.

Click **SETUP** to initialize the population. Each patch independently has an **85% probability** of receiving one citizen. This probability is fixed in the code. Realized population size varies across runs. Each citizen independently receives group 0 or 1 with equal probability.

Blue and orange indicate group membership. Squares indicate citizens meeting the imposed requirement; Xs indicate citizens required to relocate.

Set `%-similar-required` to the imposed minimum. Click **GO ONCE** for one cycle or **GO** to continue. Run SETUP when comparing fresh scenarios at different minimums.

## HOW IT WORKS

### Initialization (`setup`)

The model clears the previous simulation, makes patches white, and initializes `occupied_lot?` to `false`. Selected patches become occupied and create one citizen each. Citizens receive a group and its corresponding color. Every citizen evaluates the requirement and updates its shape. Finally, `reset-ticks` initializes the clock.

### Simulation (`go`)

At the start of each cycle, the model stops if all citizens meet the requirement. Otherwise, citizens currently below it attempt relocation in random order. After the relocation stage, all citizens reevaluate their neighbourhoods and update their shapes. The clock advances by one tick.

A citizen’s move can change other citizens’ neighbourhoods. Compliance is reevaluated after the relocation stage, not after each individual move. A tick has no assigned calendar duration.

### Relocation (`move`)

The citizen identifies all patches with `occupied_lot? = false`. If any exist, it selects one randomly, marks its origin vacant, moves to the destination, and marks the destination occupied. This maintains one citizen per patch.

The destination is selected for vacancy, not for its same-group composition. If no vacant patch exists, the citizen stays in place.

### Neighbourhood counts (`neighborhood-counts`)

This citizen reporter returns a two-item list: the number of same-group neighbours and the total number of neighbouring citizens. It examines surrounding patches, excluding the citizen’s own patch. In the bounded world, edge and corner patches have fewer neighbours.

Both evaluation and the pooled similarity indicator use this reporter. Counts are calculated when requested rather than stored as citizen attributes.

### Requirement evaluation (`evaluate`)

A citizen meets the requirement when:

    similar-nearby >= (%-similar-required * total-nearby / 100)

The result is stored in `requirement-met?`. Empty neighbouring locations do not enter the percentage. A citizen with no occupied neighbours meets the requirement because both sides of the comparison are zero.

### Appearance (`update`)

Citizens meeting the requirement become squares; those below it become Xs. This procedure changes shape, not group or location.

## MEASURES AND VISUALIZATION

The model has two collective indicator reporters:

- **`calculate-percent-similar`** pools same-group neighbour counts across citizens and divides by the pooled total neighbour count, multiplied by 100. Citizens with more neighbours contribute more weight. It reports zero if there are no neighbour observations.
- **`calculate-percent-required-to-relocate`** reports the percentage of citizens with `requirement-met? = false`. It reports zero if there are no citizens.

Use these reporters in monitors, plot pens, and BehaviorSpace. Similarity measures local same-group association; it is not a complete description of spatial segregation. Zero required relocation means everyone meets the rule, not that the city is integrated.

## SAVING FINAL LOCATIONS

### Why save locations?

The BehaviorSpace summary records collective outcomes. It does not preserve where individual citizens live. The procedure **`export-final-positions`** saves an endpoint snapshot so the spatial notebook can reconstruct the residential arrangement and calculate segregation indices.

This snapshot records final locations only. It does not record movement paths, intermediate configurations, or the number of moves made by each citizen.

### When to save

Enter the following in BehaviorSpace’s **Post run commands**:

    export-final-positions

BehaviorSpace calls it once at the end of each run, including runs ending at the time limit. Defining the procedure in the Code tab alone does not make it run. Neither `setup` nor `go` calls it automatically.

### Filename and contents

The procedure uses the BehaviorSpace run number and final tick to construct a filename such as:

    positions-run-1-tick-12.csv

This identifies run 1 ending at tick 12. The file has the header:

    run,minimum,ticks,citizen,group,x,y

- `run`: BehaviorSpace run number, used to match the summary table.
- `minimum`: imposed value of `%-similar-required`.
- `ticks`: clock value when the snapshot is saved.
- `citizen`: citizen identifier, from `who`.
- `group`: group membership, 0 or 1.
- `x`: final horizontal coordinate, from `xcor`.
- `y`: final vertical coordinate, from `ycor`.

The procedure writes one row per citizen, ordered by citizen identifier, then closes the file. Vacant patches have no rows. Notebook 2 reconstructs the full grid and treats locations without a citizen as vacant. Citizen identifiers apply within a run and should not be treated as the same people across independent runs.

The filename supplies the run and endpoint tick expected by Notebook 2. The columns also preserve the minimum and group needed to interpret each location. Keep each position file with the summary table from the same experiment.

### Where files go and how existing files are protected

The code uses relative filenames, so files are written to NetLogo’s current working directory—normally the model’s directory when using a saved model. Check the destination with a small test before running the full experiment.

If a filename already exists, the procedure adds a suffix, for example:

    positions-run-1-tick-12-1.csv

It increases the suffix until it finds an unused name. This protects existing files from overwriting. However, the supplied spatial notebook expects the original unsuffixed filename. Use a fresh model/output directory for each experiment, or adapt the notebook’s file-loading logic if using suffixed files. Do not mix position files from different experiments.

### Check the exports

Before the full experiment, test one run in a separate directory so its position file does not cause a filename collision during the full experiment. Open its position CSV and confirm that the header and citizen rows are present and that the run number and tick match the summary. A complete 510-run experiment should produce **510 position CSVs plus the BehaviorSpace summary CSV**.

The files contain no random seeds or complete model configuration. Preserve the model, experiment settings, and seeds separately if exact reproduction is required.

## THINGS TO NOTICE

Does relocation change other citizens’ compliance? Does everyone meeting the requirement imply broadly mixed neighbourhoods?

Compare the map, pooled similarity, and percentage required to relocate. A settled city may remain strongly separated. A mixed-looking endpoint may still have many citizens below the imposed minimum.

## REVERSE ENGINEERING

- **A1 — Agent:** Citizen, with owned states `group` and `requirement-met?`.
- **A2 — Arena:** City, represented by patches with the owned state `occupied_lot?`.
- **A3 — Artificiality:** No separate housing market or enforcement institution is represented.
- **A4 — Array:** Citizen–City occupancy and access to vacancies; Citizen–Citizen neighbourhood relations.

Citizen procedures implement relocation, evaluation, and appearance. `neighborhood-counts` returns local information. Observer reporters calculate collective indicators, while `export-final-positions` writes the endpoint data. Exporting locations does not change the residential arrangement.

## THINGS TO TRY: A POLICY QUESTION

**Would an imposed 30% same-group minimum produce broadly mixed residential neighbourhoods in which relocation eventually stops?**

A hypothetical Residential Integration Act imposes this rule in a city with approximately **85% occupancy**. Citizens below the minimum must attempt relocation; those meeting it remain in place. A minimum is not a target composition: a citizen with 100% same-group neighbours also meets the 30% requirement.

The fictional advisers expect residential mixing to reduce the perceived risk of collective mobilization against a dictatorial regime. The simulation tests the residential mechanism on which that argument depends; it does not test revolt risk.

### Questions to investigate

1. Does the proposed 30% minimum produce broadly mixed neighbourhoods?
2. Does relocation eventually stop? Does everyone meeting the rule imply integration?
3. How do lower and higher minimums affect residential separation and the time needed to settle?
4. Can a mixed-looking city still have many citizens required to relocate?
5. What recommendation would you make about the proposed rule? What can this model establish about the advisers’ claimed reduction in revolt risk?

### Run BehaviorSpace

Initial occupancy probability is fixed at **85% in the code**; no density parameter needs to be added to BehaviorSpace. Use the **51 × 51 world without wrapping**. Test minimums from **10% to 90% in five-point steps**, with **30 repetitions** per minimum: **510 runs**.

**Setup commands** — enter these on separate lines:

    set-topology false false
    setup

**Go commands:**

    go

**Stop condition:**

    all? citizens [requirement-met?]

**Time limit:** 1000.

**Repetitions:** 30.

**Post run commands:**

    export-final-positions

**Run metrics every step:** leave unchecked.

Parameter specification:

    ["%-similar-required" [10 5 90]]

Final measurements, one per line:

    calculate-percent-similar
    calculate-percent-required-to-relocate
    ticks
    all? citizens [requirement-met?]

Save the BehaviorSpace table as `policy-relocation-table.csv`. Keep it with the matching position files. Check that the summary contains **510 distinct runs**, **17 minimums**, and **30 repetitions per minimum**, with one matching position file for every run.

Use **01_Classic_Policy_Analysis.ipynb** to analyze summary outcomes and **02_Spatial_Clustering_PySAL.ipynb** to examine spatial separation. The supplied notebooks load repository data; point their loading cells to your experiment’s summary and position files when analyzing your own runs.

Compare variation across repetitions and distinguish settled runs from unresolved endpoints at the limit. Use both analyses to answer the policy questions.

## NETLOGO FEATURES AND MODEL LIMITS

`sprout-citizens` creates citizens on patches. `ask` executes selected agents in random order. `one-of` selects a vacant destination randomly, and explicit occupancy updates preserve the one-citizen-per-patch constraint.

The local variables declared with `let` exist only during the executing procedure. They are not persistent citizen attributes. The two indicator reporters guard against division by zero.

Use the final `all? citizens [requirement-met?]` measurement to identify settled runs. A run meeting the requirement at tick 1,000 is settled at the limit; a run ending there with `false` remains unresolved.

The `go` procedure stops when everyone meets the requirement. The 1,000-step cap is a BehaviorSpace setting, not a stopping rule inside `go`.

The model assumes obedience and unrestricted access to vacant locations. It omits moving burdens, housing costs, discrimination, and enforcement processes. Meeting the requirement does not demonstrate integration, personal approval, or political stability. Capped endpoints do not establish equilibrium.

## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

This teaching adaptation defines an explicit Citizen breed, separates group membership from color, introduces patch occupancy states, and replaces the original movement search with direct relocation to randomly selected vacant patches.

The adaptation separates neighbourhood evaluation, relocation, and appearance updates into Citizen procedures, while macro-level indicators are calculated through observer-level reporters. This policy version uses an imposed requirement rather than personal satisfaction and exports final citizen locations for spatial analysis.

The reverse-engineering scaffold and proposed experiments are teaching adaptations, rather than claims made by the original authors.

Schelling, T. C. (1969). Models of Segregation. *The American Economic Review, 59*(2), 488–493.

Schelling, T. (1978). *Micromotives and Macrobehavior*. New York: Norton.

The 1969 reading provides the theoretical discussion. Its line example differs from this NetLogo grid implementation.

See also: Rauch, J. (2002). Seeing Around Corners. *The Atlantic Monthly*, April 2002, 289(4), 35–48. https://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/archive/2002/04/seeing-around-corners/302471/

## HOW TO CITE

For the original model:

- Wilensky, U. (1997). *NetLogo Segregation model*. http://ccl.northwestern.edu/netlogo/models/Segregation. Center for Connected Learning and Computer-Based Modeling, Northwestern University, Evanston, IL.

For the software:

- Wilensky, U. (1999). *NetLogo*. http://ccl.northwestern.edu/netlogo/. Center for Connected Learning and Computer-Based Modeling, Northwestern University, Evanston, IL.

When describing this version, also identify it as a teaching adaptation and explain the changes used in your analysis.

## AUTHORSHIP AND ACKNOWLEDGMENTS

Prof. Jose Magallanes prepared this teaching model for an agent-based modeling course, adapting code from Uri Wilensky’s NetLogo Segregation model (1997).

This version introduces an explicit Citizen breed, separates group membership from color, tracks residential occupancy through patch states, and implements direct relocation to randomly selected vacant locations.

It also separates citizen relocation, neighbourhood evaluation, and appearance updates into distinct methods, calculates macro-level indicators through observer-level reporters, and includes a reverse-engineering teaching approach and a proposed BehaviorSpace policy experiment.

The model draws on Thomas Schelling’s work on how individual residential preferences can generate collective segregation.

This course version is distinct from the model distributed in the NetLogo Models Library. The original code attribution and license remain below.

## COPYRIGHT AND LICENSE

Copyright 1997 Uri Wilensky.

![CC BY-NC-SA 3.0](http://ccl.northwestern.edu/images/creativecommons/byncsa.png)

